N2  - Abstract Background: Prolonged postsurgical recovery or postoperative ileus is a huge burden for hospitals and patients, and requires effective therapies. The aims of this study was to evaluate the effects and possible mechanisms of transcutaneous electrical acustimulation (TEA) in enhancing postoperative recovery after open appendectomy. Methods: Forty-five patients diagnosed with appendicitis and undergoing open appendectomy were randomized to three groups: TEA group received TEA during intraoperative period and from postoperative day (POD) 1 to POD3, P-TEA group received TEA only during intraoperative period, or sham-TEA group received sham-TEA (same electrical stimulation at non-acupoints) during intraoperative period and from POD1 to POD3. TEA after surgery was performed at the bilateral Zusanli (ST36) (1 h, daily). Intraoperative TEA was performed from start to finish. Autonomic function was assessed from spectral analysis of heart rate variability derived from the ECG. The primary outcome was the time to first passing flatus after surgery. Secondary outcomes included time to defecation, time to ambulation, time to resume diet, duration of hospital stays, and visual analogue scale (VAS) score for pain from POD1 to POD3. Results: (1) Patients in TEA group had a shorter time to first passing flatus than those of P-TEA and sham-TEA group (17.00±6.25 vs 21.87±5.98; p=0.049; or vs 24.73±7.43; p=0.002); (2) Patients in TEA group had a shorter time to defecation than those of sham-TEA group (32.17± 12.59 vs 41.93±11.65; p=0.012); (3) TEA enhanced the vagal activity from POD1 to POD3 (0.30±0.13 vs 0.50±0.21; p=0.003); (4) P-TEA showed no effects on any of the measurements in comparison with sham-TEA. Conclusion: TEA reduced the time to first passing flatus and time to defecation, compared with P-TEA or sham-TEA. The treatment of the daily needleless TEA was useful for quickening postoperative recovery after open appendectomy. Autonomic mechanism (suppression of surgically) might be involved in the therapeutic effect.
