N2  - Aim: Ample evidence suggested that 'fast-track' (FT) programme accelerates postoperative recovery. Our recent study also demonstrated that electroacupuncture (EA) can enhance recovery after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. This randomised noninferiority trial aimed to compare the efficacy of EA and FT programme in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. Method: Between 01/2014 and 03/2016, 164 patients undergoing elective laparoscopic colorectal surgery were randomised to receive either EA or FT programme. The primary outcome was time to defaecation. Results: The mean time to defaecation in the EA and FT groups was 79 and 72.9 hrs (difference = 6.1 hrs; 95% CI ±5.2 hrs to 17.5 hrs), respectively (P = 0.286). Noninferiority was demonstrated as the upper limit of 95% CI for the difference was within the prespecified noninferiority margin of 24 hrs. The mean length of hospital stay of the EA and FT groups was also similar (5.6 vs 6.6 days, P = 0.172). Fewer patients in the EA group developed prolonged ileus (4.9% vs 14.6%, P = 0.06). Conclusion: EA is noninferior to FT programme in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. EA may be the preferred perioperative therapy for laparoscopic colorectal surgery because it is simpler to implement and less labour intensive than FT programme.
