N2  - Backgrounds: Opioid is widely used in cancer pain, but long-term use may leads to tolerance. This research is to explore the efficacy of electroacupuncture treatment in cancer induced bone pain (CIBP) rat model with morphine tolerance. To analyze the expression of calcitonin-gene related peptide (CGRP) immuno-histochemisty in dorsal root ganglion (DRG). Methods: Forty SD rats were plan to divided into four groups: sham, CIBP + morphine tolerance (CM), CIBP + electroacupuncture (CE), and CIBP + morphine tolerance + electroacupuncture (CME). CM, CE and CME groups were prepared CIBP model by carcinoma cell tibia implanted. Sham only accepted sham operation without carcinoma cell implanted. After six days later when CIBP model had succeeded, we continued to make pathology of morphine tolerance. The three CIBP models accepted different treatment separately: morphine intrathecal administration, electroacupuncture treatment alone, morphine combined electroacupuncture, nine days treatment continuously in every group. Acupoints were selected Zusanli (ST36) and Sanyinjiao (SP6) bilateral. Electroacupuncture treatment was manipulated by 2/50 Hz frequency, 20min bid, 9 days continuously. 50% mechanical withdraw threshold was evaluated by von Frey filament stimulation. CGRP expression in DRG was detected by immunohistochemisty. Results: After five days of morphine administration, evidence of morphine tolerance appeared by pain mechanical threshold increasing. At the end of the study, pain threshold was 10.9g in CME group, compared with 8.7g in CM group and 6.2g in CE group (P < 0.01, separately). IOD value of CGRP expression in dorsal root ganglion was 9026.5 in CME group, compared with 14803.1 in CM group and 15730.6 in CE group (P < 0.01, separately). Conclusion: Electroacupuncture can relieve morphine tolerance in CIBP rat. The mechanism is related to CGRP overdue expression inhibited in DRG.
