N2  - Background: Colon dysfunction after spinal cord injury (SCI) is one of the important factors that affect the patients' life quality. The effect of acupuncture exerts effect on colon dysfunction after spinal cord injury, without any obvious side effects, but the mechanisms have not been well elucidated yet. Purpose: Clock genes of colon are the key to adjust its rhythmic movement, which can play a role without the central regulation of suprachiasmatic nucleus. This study aims to exam whether acupuncture can promote rehabilitation of colonic dysfunction after SCI through recovering colon rhythmic movement and transmission by regulation at per2 gene. Methods: SD rats were randomized into sham-operation group, model group, electroacupuncture (EA) group, and drug group. The colon dysfunction rat model of T11-12 spinal cord injury was generated by modified Allen's impact method.EAwith dilatationalwave was applied toEAgroup at acupoint “Zusanli” (ST40) for 30 minutes. Drug group was treated by oral administration of mosaprid. The treatments were carried once daily and last for 14 days. Colon rhythmic movement and transmission was evaluated by the time of first black stool excretion and the number of waste grain within 4 h. The colonic tissue structure was observed afterHE staining. ThemRNAexpression of per2was measured byRTPCR analysis. The protein expression of per2 was detected by western blot and immunohistochemistry method. Results: Compared with sham-operation group, the time of first black stool excretion in model rat was significantly prolonged, and waste grain number within 4 h was reduced significantly. Our results showed electroacupuncture can shorten the time of first black stool excretion, and increase the number of waste grain within 4 h, at the same time. Those effects were more significantly than mosaprid. Morphology observation of colonic tissue after HE staining showed that after modeling, colonic wall atrophy, mucosal erosion with inflammatory cells invasion, inherent gland reduced, smooth muscle thickness obviously, and interstitial mild edema. Whereas this situation of the colonic smooth muscle in the EAgroup was close to that of the sham-operation group. Both gene and protein expression of per2 were elevated in colonic tissue of model group rats, those are verified by RT-PCR and Western blot. EA treatment can reduce gene and protein expression of per2 more respectively than mosaprid treatment. Immunohistochemical result showed that per2 protein was positive expressed at colonic myenteric plexus mainly. Per2 protein expressions in immuno-reaction were consistent with Western blot analysis. Conclusion(s): Our study demonstrates for the first time that acupuncture can effectively improve rhythmicmovement and transmission of colon after spinal cord injury, recover colonic function. The effect mechanism of acupuncture treatment may be responsible for its efficacy in benign regulation at per2 expression at the level of gene and protein. It is important to find the pathway, which is acupuncture treatment regulates at clock gene per2 through. It can clarify acupuncture treatment action mechanism further more. Implications: Our study is looking forward to reveal the molecular mechanism of acupuncture treating colon dysfunction after spinal cord injury, so as to guide clinical practice.
