N2  - Aim: Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation on acupoints (Acu- TENS) is a non-invasive modality that has been used successfully to treat pain of various origins. This study aimed to investigate the efficacy of Acu-TENS in reducing pain and consumption of sedatives/analgesics during colonoscopy. Method: Between 10/2012 and 12/2013, 135 patients undergoing firsttime elective colonoscopy were randomised to receive either Acu-TENS (n = 68) or placebo-TENS (n = 67) before/during colonoscopy. The acupoints Zusanli, Hegu, and Neiguan were used. A mixture of propofol and alfentanil, delivered by a patient-controlled syringe pump, was used for sedation/analgesia in both groups. The primary outcome was the dose of patient-controlled sedation/analgesia consumed. Secondary outcomes included pain/satisfaction scores and caecal intubation time. Results: The baseline characteristics of the two groups were comparable. The mean dose of propofol used was significantly lower in the Acu- TENS group than in the placebo-TENS group (0.25 vs 0.88 mg/kg; P < 0.001). Comparing with the placebo-TENS group, the Acu-TENS group had a lower mean pain score and a higher mean satisfaction score. A trend towards shorter caecal intubation time also was observed in the Acu-TENS group (10.2 vs 12.3 min; P = 0.061). Multiple linear regression analysis revealed that the use of Acu-TENS, shorter procedure time, absence of looping, older age, and male gender were independent predictors of less consumption of sedatives/analgesics during colonoscopy. Conclusion: This study suggests that Acu-TENS is more effective than placebo-TENS in reducing pain and consumption of sedatives/analgesics during colonoscopy. The use of Acu-TENS is an independent predictor of less consumption of patient-controlled sedatives/analgesics during colonoscopy.
