N2  - Objective: To observe the behavioral function as well as expressions of glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) and stage-specific embryonic antigen-1 (SSEA-1) at spinal cord injury site in rats treated by electro-acupuncture (EA) for spinal cord injury so as to elucidate the mechanisms of EA on the proliferation and differentiation of reactive astrocytes after spinal cord injury. Methods: Sixty adult SD rats were randomly divided into sham-operation group, model group and EA treatment group, with 20 in each group. At day 3, 7, 14 and 28 after spinal cord injury modeling, Basso-Beaie-Bresnehan (BBB) scale was used; the changes of GFAP and SSEA-1 expressions at spinal cord injury site were detected by immunohistochemical method in the three groups. Results: In model group and EA treatment group, the number of GFAP and SSEA-1 positive cells differed at days 3 and 7 (P<0.01), reached the peak at day 7, and differed significantly at day 14 (P<0.05). However, at day 28, the two groups differed obviously in SSEA-1 positive cells count (P<0.05), but not in GFAP positive cells count (P≤0.05). Conclusion: Electro-acupuncture treatment can promote the inverse differentiation of astrocytes of rats with spinal cord injury, stimulate the potential of astrocytes' stem cells to promote the recovery of spinal cord function after injury. GFAP and SSEA-1 positive expressions at spinal cord injury site both peak at day 7, suggesting that electro-acupuncture intervention within seven days of spinal cord injury may be the optimal time.
